.TH SNIPPING-TOOL 1 snipping-tool\-VERSION
.SH NAME
snipping-tool \- screen snips with pen, highlighter and OCR
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B snipping-tool
.RB [ \-flwtsnhv ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR sec ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-i
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-g ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B snipping-tool
takes a snip of the screen and puts it on the clipboard as
.IR image/png .
The screen is frozen first and shown under a white veil; the part to keep
is selected with the mouse.
.P
Without options it works on the command line: drag a rectangle (a click
without dragging takes the window under the pointer) and the snip goes to
the clipboard, like
.IP
scrot \-s \-F \- | xclip \-selection clipboard \-t image/png
.P
The process that owns the clipboard stays in the background until another
program takes the clipboard, so the snip can be pasted after
.B snipping-tool
returns.
.P
With
.BR \-g " or " \-\-graphic
it starts the window that looks like the Windows Snipping Tool: the
.B New
button with its snip types (free-form, rectangle, window, full screen),
options, and after a snip the markup window with a pen, a highlighter, an
eraser that removes whole strokes, saving, copying, sending by mail and
text recognition.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-graphic
start the graphical version. With any other option
.B \-g
is ignored, so that an alias that adds it does not get in the way.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-full
take the whole screen, no selection.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-window
take the active window with its frame, no selection.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-free
free-form selection: draw around the part to keep, the rest becomes white.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-save
also save the snip into $HOME; the file name is printed.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " \fIfile\fP"
also save the snip into
.IR file ;
.B \-
writes it to the standard output.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-delay " \fIsec\fP"
wait
.I sec
seconds before the screen is frozen.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-text
recognize the text of the snip (OCR): print it and put it on the clipboard
instead of the image.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-copy
do not touch the clipboard.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-input " \fIfile\fP"
take the image from a PNG
.I file
instead of the screen (useful with
.BR \-t ).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
print usage.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
print version.
.SH SELECTION
.TP
.B Button1
drag to select, click a window to take it.
.TP
.BR Escape ", " Button3
cancel.
.SH GRAPHICAL VERSION KEYS
.TP
.B Ctrl\-n
new snip.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-s
save as PNG.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-c
copy the snip with the marks.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-z
undo the last mark.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-t
recognize text.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-q
quit.
.TP
.B F1
help.
.TP
.B Alt\-key, F10
menus and buttons by their underlined letter.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/snipping-tool/config
settings of the Options dialog (also read by the command line version for
the veil and the ink color).
.SH CUSTOMIZATION
.B snipping-tool
is customized by creating a custom config.h and (re)compiling the source
code. Icons are PNG files in icons/ converted with
.BR "make icons" .
.SH OCR
The recognizer is meant for text rendered on screen in common fonts,
Latin and Cyrillic, 10 to 18 pixels and more. Italics, handwriting and
very small or decorative fonts are not recognized well.
.SH EXAMPLES
dwm, take a rectangle with Print and the active window with Shift-Print:
.IP
{ 0, XK_Print, spawn, SHCMD("snipping-tool") },
.br
{ ShiftMask, XK_Print, spawn, SHCMD("snipping-tool \-w") },
.P
Text of a part of the screen into the clipboard:
.IP
snipping-tool \-t
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR scrot (1),
.BR xclip (1),
.BR gocr (1)
.SH BUGS
X11 only; under Wayland the screen of XWayland applications is all it can
see.
